# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 7 - Recepy for Loop Invariants - exercises", Exercise 2; exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 3 a).
id: factorial
title: Factorial, with the recipe
topic: Loop invariants
level: 2
source: Slides 7, Recipe exercises, Exercise 2; exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 3 a
tags: recipe, invariant, factorial

task:
  Use the recipe to find the invariant $I$. Check establishment and
  preservation, show that $Q$ holds at the exit, and give a variant.

  Hint: look at the order of the two statements in the body. The variable
  $i$ is increased before it is used.

pre: n >= 0
post: f = fact(n)

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  i := 0;            // i1
  {R3}
  f := 1;            // i2
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    i := i + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    f := f * i       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Trace and generalise
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  | At the loop head | $i$ | $f$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $0$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $1$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $2$ | $2$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $3$ | $6$ |

  The values are $f = i!$. Replacing $n$ by $i$ in $f = n!$ gives the same
  formula. With the bounds of $i$:
set: I = f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n
note:
  If you wrote $f = (i + 1)!$ or $f = (i - 1)!$, check the trace: at the
  first loop head $i = 0$ and $f = 1 = 0!$.

step: Establishment
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, WP
focus: i1, i2
calc:
  wp(f := 1, I)
  = 1 = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n
set: R3 = 1 = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R3)
  = 1 = fact(0) && 0 <= 0 <= n
  = 0 <= n                         // 0! = 1
set: R4 = 0 <= n
vc: n >= 0 ==> 0 <= n

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, I)
  = f * i = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n
set: R1 = f * i = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
set: R2 = f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
vc: f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
why:
  $(i + 1)! = i! * (i + 1)$, and $f = i!$, so $f * (i + 1) = (i + 1)!$. The
  bounds follow from $0 <= i$ and $i < n$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n && !(i < n) ==> f = fact(n)
why:
  $i >= n$ and $i <= n$ give $i = n$, so $f = n!$.
result: The triple is valid. A variant for termination is $n - i$.
